    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nOctet completion in aluminium halides.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nAlCl3 has 6 electrons in valence shell of Al (electron deficient).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nTwo AlCl3 units share lone pairs from chlorine atoms to form Al2Cl6 dimer.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. In monomeric AlCl3, aluminium has only 6 valence electrons (incomplete octet).\n2. To complete its octet, each Al atom coordinates with a lone pair from a Cl atom of an adjacent AlCl3 unit.\n3. This forms a bridged dimer Al2Cl6 with two 3c-4e coordinate bonds.\n4. In aqueous solution, hydration energy overcomes the lattice/dimer energy, forming [Al(H2O)6]3+.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: A dimeric molecule Al2Cl6 with halogen bridging\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nAlCl3 forms a covalent dimer Al2Cl6 in vapour and non-polar solvents, but hydrolyzes into ions in water.",
