    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nInert pair effect in heavier p-block elements.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nGroup 13 elements (B, Al, Ga, In, Tl).\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nRelativistic contraction and poor shielding of 4f/5d electrons stabilizes the outer ns2 electrons.\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Down the group, the intervening d and f electrons shield the outer ns electrons poorly.\n2. Consequently, the ns2 electrons are held tightly by the nucleus and do not easily participate in bonding (inert pair effect).\n3. Therefore, stability of the +1 oxidation state increases down Group 13: B+ < Al+ < Ga+ < In+ < Tl+.\n4. Tl(I) is exceptionally stable, while Tl(III) acts as a strong oxidizing agent.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: Tl+ > In+ > Ga+ > Al+ > B+\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE WRONG:\nLower oxidation state (+1) becomes more stable down the group, while group state (+3) becomes less stable.",
